  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Sunday: Scattered Light Rain Showers\n\nAn H500 shortwave trough is moving over the region today and is \naiding in developing light rain showers across Iowa and Minnesota. \nMost of the activity today should stay to the west of the \nMississippi River, where the higher moisture and stronger forcing \nare located. Any rain amounts for today should be very light, the \nHREF has around a 20-50% chance for rainfall to exceed a quarter \ninch near I-35 and into the far western portions of the CWA by \nSunday morning. Scattered showers may develop further east overnight \ninto Wisconsin, but amounts should remain light as the moisture axis \nshifts slowly to the east. Rain showers should decrease in coverage \nby late Sunday morning as synoptic scale lift decreases over the \nUpper Midwest. Temperatures both today and Sunday should mainly be \nin the 60s, with some locations reaching the lower 70s. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Dry Monday - Rain Chances Return Tuesday\n\nThe forecast area will be in between two different weather systems \nto start the next work week. A low pressure system will continue to \nremain over the northeastern US and a trough will dig south across \nthe western CONUS. Pleasant conditions overall are expected on \nMonday with temperatures in the lower 70s and partly cloudy skies \nwith very little wind. As the aforementioned trough continues to dig \nsouth over the Desert Southwest on Tuesday, moisture should surge \nnorthward out ahead of this feature. Sufficient PVA supplied by \nshortwaves embedded in the upper level flow should lead to rain \nshowers and potentially thunderstorms developing across the region \non Tuesday into Wednesday morning. Just how much rain we receive \nfrom the initial bands of showers and storms will be dependent upon \nthe moisture quality going into Wednesday morning. The 90th \npercentile rainfall amounts from the LREF do indicate the potential \nfor some locations to see an inch of rainfall or more, but \nconfidence is low at this time.\n\nRest of Next Week: Daily Rain Chances May Continue\n\nThe remainder of this forecast period is highly uncertain as it will \ndepend upon the evolution of the low pressure center associated with \nthe synoptic scale trough as well as how this feature combines with \nthe remnants of now Hurricane Polo. Ensemble guidance along with \ndeterministic forecast models indicate plenty of differences in \ntiming and location in the axis of better rain chances for Wednesday \nand beyond. This is shown really well in the NBM PoP values, which \nkeep at least low to medium (20-50%) chances for rain through \nFriday. The current expectation is that the heaviest rainfall will \nstay south of the Upper Midwest, but there are outlier solutions \nthat produce 2+ inches of rainfall during the later half of this \nupcoming week. Temperatures are forecast to remain near average to \nend the month of September.",
